[Music]  I'm Darren George and I grow and restore  bonsai trees I'm going to walk you  through how I restore an overgrown  bonsai tree the tree that I'm working on  today is called cotoniasta this  particular tree was started by a bonsai  professional up north in England and  came into my possession when the  previous owner decided to give up Bonsai  and had let the tree deteriorate  somewhat the tree has fallen into  disrepair it has several overgrown  branches and there are also a number of  branches that have completely died the  dead wood is rotting and falling away  from the tree  foreign  the first step in restoring this bonsai  tree is going to be cleaning out the  massive branches so that I can see the  underlying structure of the tree I'm  going to remove any dead pieces and  generally thin out the massive leaves  and twigs  now I can see what's going on inside of  the canopy I can see that there are a  couple of branches that are growing from  the same position they're crowding each  other out and making the tree look quite  messy and spoiling the flow of the  design so I'm going to select one or two  branches to completely remove to open up  some space to ensure the health of the  branches that remain and also create a  more open and rounded feel to the canopy  where I am removing a larger Branch I'm  going to use a special putty that we  call cut paste the cut paste helps the  wound heal much faster than it  ordinarily would and prevents rot and  bacteria setting in  where I've created some space in the  canopy I'm going to use some wire to  reposition a couple of the remaining  branches so that we've got quite a nice  even distribution of foliage around the  tree and remove any large gaps in the  canopy and once they're all in position  the work for this moment is complete and  I can allow the tree to respond and grow  into the new shape  several months later in the spring and  the state of the Deadwood is really  starting to bother me the longer I leave  it the more it's going to deteriorate so  I'd like to get the dead wood treated  and hardened before I apply the  treatment to the dead wood I have to  ensure that the dead wood is Thoroughly  dry I keep it out of the elements under  shelter so that it can dry out I'm using  a two-part epoxy resin it has a solvent  mixed in with it that encourages the  resin to penetrate the soft dead wood so  that the epoxy can Harden further into  the core of the wood and hopefully allow  it to last longer  I'm applying the epoxy resin to the dead  wood as carefully as I can trying to  ensure not to get it on the live wood or  on the soil  I'm going to let it dry for a couple of  days  now that epoxy is completely dry I'm  rigorously brushing it to take the sheen  off the surface of the wood I really  want it to look old and Sun bleached  rather than shiny and new so using a  steel brush it roughs up the surface and  takes that reflection off the wood  foreign  [Music]  this tree has absolutely exploded with  growth the branches are super long super  thick and really really healthy once  again I need to come along I need to cut  back these branches to regain the shape  of the canopy I'm also checking for any  opportunities to reposition any branches  that aren't in the ideal locations  so with all these really long shoots  that have grown over summer I'm going to  trim them back to regain the silhouette  and it also gives me another opportunity  to apply some wire to reposition any new  branches that have developed since and  continue filling in the tree's crown  now with all the watering that I've been  doing over the hot summer we've got some  hard water stains built up around the  base of the tree on the bottom of the  trunk and on some of the roots on the  soil surface  I'm using a toothbrush and vinegar to  try and dissolve and scrub off some of  that white calcium buildup  on the summer surface there are some  undissolved fertilizer pellets I'll  quickly brush those off to reveal the  green moss underneath  and that concludes the work on this tree  now I'm done with this Bonsai I'm really  excited to see how it responds and  continue working with it as it grows  into the future  foreign

